ADR Reporting & Documentation

1. Case Information

Case ID: ADR-002
Patient: 45-year-old female
Age: 45 years
Sex: Female
Indication: Bacterial respiratory tract infection

2. Suspected Medication

Drug Name: Amoxicillin
Strength: 500 mg
Dosage: 500 mg three times daily
Route: Oral
Duration: 5 days
Therapeutic Indication: Treatment of bacterial respiratory tract infection

3. Adverse Drug Reaction

Reaction: Skin rash, itching and mild facial swelling
Date of Onset: Day 3 after starting amoxicillin
Description: The patient developed generalized skin rash and itching, followed by mild facial swelling.

4. Action Taken

Amoxicillin was discontinued after the adverse reaction was identified. The patient was monitored and received appropriate symptomatic treatment.

5. Dechallenge

Positive. The patient's symptoms gradually improved after discontinuation of amoxicillin.

6. Rechallenge

Not performed. Re-exposure was avoided because of the suspected hypersensitivity reaction.

7. Concomitant Medication

Paracetamol 500 mg, taken as needed for fever.

8. Outcome

The patient's symptoms improved after withdrawal of the suspected drug. The patient was advised to avoid further exposure to amoxicillin unless specifically evaluated by a healthcare professional.

9. Seriousness Assessment

Seriousness: Non-serious
Severity: Moderate
Hospitalization: No
Death: No
Life-threatening: No

10. Causality Assessment

The temporal relationship between amoxicillin administration and the onset of the reaction was reasonable. The symptoms improved after withdrawal of the suspected drug, and no significant alternative cause was identified.

WHO-UMC Causality Category: Probable/Likely

11. ADR Classification

Type: Type B (Bizarre)
Predictability: Not predictable
Preventability: Probably preventable

12. Reporter Information

Reporter: Pharmacovigilance Trainee
Reporting Date: 25 September 2026
Report Type: Initial ADR Report

13. Summary

A 45-year-old female developed skin rash, itching, and mild facial swelling on Day 3 of amoxicillin therapy. The suspected drug was discontinued, after which the patient's symptoms gradually improved. Based on the temporal relationship, positive dechallenge, and absence of a more likely alternative explanation, the reaction was assessed as Probable/Likely according to the WHO-UMC causality assessment system.
